WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.620
Why is knapsack dynamic programming

2
00:00:02.620 --> 00:00:05.100
only pseudo polynomial? We will

3
00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:06.900
distinguish a numeric value from

4
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.220
the number of bits encoding

5
00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:10.360
it, then compare knapsack and

6
00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.000
coin change with longest common

7
00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:12.740
subsequence.

8
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.420
A polynomial-time claim is measured

9
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:16.520
against the length of the

10
00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:19.100
input representation. Writing a string

11
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:21.100
of n characters explicitly supplies

12
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.680
n symbols, but a binary

13
00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:24.180
integer can represent a much

14
00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.820
larger numeric value in only

15
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:26.540
a few bits.

16
00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:30.440
For example, binary one thousand

17
00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:32.360
is the number eight, represented

18
00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:35.280
by four bits. A capacity-eight

19
00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:36.880
table uses entries up to

20
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:38.860
eight even though the capacity

21
00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:40.780
field itself has only four

22
00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:41.540
written symbols.

23
00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.760
With b bits, the largest

24
00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:45.580
unsigned capacity is two to

25
00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.160
the b minus one. A

26
00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:48.960
table indexed through every capacity

27
00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:51.320
may therefore require exponentially many

28
00:00:51.320 --> 00:00:53.320
entries in b, despite being

29
00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:54.740
linear in the value C.

30
00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:57.740
The zero-one knapsack table has

31
00:00:57.740 --> 00:00:59.380
n item choices and C

32
00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:01.720
capacity values, giving big O

33
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:03.440
of n C work. If

34
00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:04.700
C is near two to

35
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:06.600
the b, that expression becomes

36
00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:08.420
order n times two to

37
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:10.180
the b, not a polynomial

38
00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:11.560
in n and b.

39
00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:14.260
We call this pseudo polynomial

40
00:01:14.260 --> 00:01:16.620
time. The runtime is polynomial

41
00:01:16.620 --> 00:01:18.900
in the numeric capacity, without

42
00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:20.420
a polynomial bound in the

43
00:01:20.420 --> 00:01:23.240
encoded input length. This describes

44
00:01:23.240 --> 00:01:25.420
the given algorithm and representation,

45
00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:27.260
not a claim that every

46
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:29.560
input always takes its largest

47
00:01:29.560 --> 00:01:30.640
possible table.

48
00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:33.320
Minimum-coin DP has the same

49
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:34.660
issue with its target amount

50
00:01:34.660 --> 00:01:36.450
A. Big O of k

51
00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:37.940
A depends on all amounts

52
00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:39.480
up to A, while the

53
00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:41.380
binary target field occupies only

54
00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:42.620
order log A bits.

55
00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.880
Longest common subsequence is different

56
00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:47.540
because its two strings are

57
00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:50.260
explicitly listed. Their lengths n

58
00:01:50.260 --> 00:01:52.440
and m already contribute n

59
00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.100
plus m input symbols, so

60
00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:56.360
the n m table is

61
00:01:56.360 --> 00:01:57.960
polynomial in that total length.

62
00:01:58.420 --> 00:02:00.060
The symbols n and m

63
00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:01.520
alone do not decide the

64
00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:04.160
classification. You must state whether

65
00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:05.880
a parameter measures an explicit

66
00:02:05.880 --> 00:02:08.040
sequence length, a numeric amount,

67
00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.080
or an encoding length before

68
00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:11.680
interpreting its appearance in a

69
00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:11.940
runtime.

70
00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:14.760
Our operation counts use the

71
00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.280
course assumption of constant-time arithmetic

72
00:02:17.280 --> 00:02:19.560
on suitable machine words. For

73
00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:21.540
enormous exact integers, the cost

74
00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:23.480
of addition, comparison and storage

75
00:02:23.480 --> 00:02:25.060
also depends on bit length,

76
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.600
which should be included in

77
00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:28.240
a full bit-operation analysis.

78
00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:31.120
An exact knapsack DP therefore

79
00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:32.680
does not establish that the

80
00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:34.420
knapsack decision problem has a

81
00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.900
polynomial-time algorithm in binary input

82
00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:39.560
size. It removes repeated subproblems

83
00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:41.020
effectively, but the number of

84
00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:42.800
capacity states still carries the

85
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:44.080
numeric-magnitude dependence.

86
00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:46.080
Things to take note of:

87
00:02:46.460 --> 00:02:48.400
separate numeric values from encoding

88
00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.320
lengths and substitute the bit-length

89
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:52.620
relationship before classifying a bound.

90
00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.180
LCS is polynomial in its

91
00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:57.380
explicit string lengths, whereas the

92
00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:59.460
capacity and amount DPs depend

93
00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:01.800
on numeric magnitudes; a table

94
00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:03.480
can be neatly organised and

95
00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:05.580
still be very large, hmph.
